    "question": {
      ":question/id": "fb566793-0341-5254-9104-5c57da86475d",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Simplify.\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\sin\\left(49^\\circ\\right)\\cos\\left(49^\\circ\\right)}{\\cos\\left(98^\\circ\\right)}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Put $x=49^\\circ$. Recall $2\\sin x\\cos x=\\sin(2x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2\\sin x\\cos x}{\\cos(2x)} &=\\frac{2\\sin x\\cos x}{\\cos(2x)} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sin(2x)}{\\cos(2x)} \\\\ &=\\tan(2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is nonzero, and $\\tan(2x)=\\sin(2x)/\\cos(2x)$.\n\nSubstitute $x=49^\\circ$ and double the whole angle: $2x=98^\\circ$. The result is\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(98^\\circ\\right) .\n$$",
