    "question": {
      ":question/id": "65ee3586-c6ef-5e4e-8675-b19299777955",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Simplify.\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sin\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{9}\\right)+\\cos\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{9}\\right)\\right)^2\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Put $x=\\frac{4\\pi}{9}$. Expand the square and combine the squared terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sin x+\\cos x)^2 &=\\sin^2 x+2\\sin x\\cos x+\\cos^2 x \\\\ &=1+2\\sin x\\cos x \\\\ &=1+\\sin(2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $\\sin^2 x+\\cos^2 x=1$ and $2\\sin x\\cos x=\\sin(2x)$.\n\nSubstitute $x=\\frac{4\\pi}{9}$ and double the whole angle: $2x=\\frac{8\\pi}{9}$. The result is\n\n$$\n1+\\sin\\left(\\frac{8\\pi}{9}\\right) .\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
